 @euromanijak  Do you know why I stopped reading books some five years ago or even a decade? Do you know why I mostly stopped watching movies, but then suddenly restarted — same thing with the non-sim games? That's because they were all full of their authors while not bringing anything particularly interesting to the table. Hardly ever there is a breath of fresh air. But HDR is what made it worth watching/playing at least for the visuals. It also brought back my interest in photography too, since instead of the nauseatingly flat, near-grey sRGB pictures you are getting ones that can actually start fooling your brain into believing you are looking at real sunsets and sunrises, tropical birds and flowers, precious minerals and metals - not to mention artificial night lighting.
Plot and "gameplay" is like Hard Rock/Metal — it peaked back in the '90s, and was on life support since then, but a good picture (and sound!) is timeless. These days I'm judging the games by their artistic value, and that also means more vibrant colors and much deeper brightness range. And when you have HDR, ray tracing further enhances the effect (with photography and movies you obviously don't really need that — except the deepness also very much depends on the lighting conditions).
But if you really care about the plot and plot only, it's hard to beat the books... Games only add awkward "mechanics" to the whole process and an illusion of being given "options". So, to me for a game to be interesting beyond its visuals, it needs to be a sim. The object of simulation itself becomes the "game", because things like helicopters and motorcycles are interesting to learn to handle... and even some cars and planes too. That's not to mention sail-equipped ocean-going vessels... or heck, even trains and road-trains.

